<h2>Why refund is the exception</h2>
<p>Maintenance — under Section 144 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 (the successor to Section 125 CrPC), Section 24 of the Hindu Marriage Act or Section 20 of the DV Act — is a subsistence remedy. The figure is fixed so that the dependent spouse and children can eat, live and litigate while the dispute is decided. The money is, by design, spent as it arrives. A refund direction therefore does something maintenance law is built to avoid: it converts a protective payment into a retrospective debt owed by the very person the statute presumes cannot maintain herself. That is why the working rule across maintenance forums is that sums paid under a valid interim order are not ordered to be returned merely because the final determination turned out lower or the substantive claim eventually failed.</p>

<h2>Adjustment: the law\'s preferred instrument</h2>
<p>What courts do instead is adjust. The excess emerges as a credit in the running account between the parties and is absorbed in one of three ways.</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>Against arrears</h3><p>Where the final order operates from the application date, amounts already paid for the same period are deducted before arrears are computed — the payer is never charged twice for the same months.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Against future instalments</h3><p>Where interim payments exceeded the final figure, the surplus is set off against instalments falling due under the final order or under permanent alimony fixed at divorce.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Across parallel orders</h3><p>Where orders under the DV Act, the BNSS and the HMA coexist, disclosure of each payment is required and overlapping sums are adjusted so the entitlements do not stack.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The same logic governs appellate modification. When a High Court reduces a Family Court\'s figure, the operative direction is almost always prospective adjustment of the excess rather than repayment — the appellate court shapes the remedy so that relief to the payer does not become destitution for the payee.</p>

<h2>Where recovery becomes realistic</h2>
<p>The balance shifts when the payee\'s own conduct taints the order. Three situations recur. First, the <strong>false affidavit</strong>: maintenance quantum is built on sworn disclosures of income and assets, and an order shown to rest on deliberate concealment can be altered, and payments for the affected period recovered, because the equity protecting consumption assumes honest need. Second, the <strong>suppressed disqualification</strong>: payments drawn after remarriage, or after another disqualifying event, without disclosure, for which the alteration power — Section 146 BNSS, and the variation provisions such as Section 25(2) and (3) HMA for alimony — supplies the machinery, with recovery of the post-event amounts a natural consequence. Third, the <strong>void order</strong>: where the order itself was without jurisdiction, restitutionary principles of the kind codified in Section 144 CPC have the most room to operate, since there was never a valid protective order to shield the receipts.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note for the paying side:</strong> the time to control the refund problem is at the interim stage, not after. Contest quantum with documents early, seek staged or account-routed payments, press for expeditious final disposal, and where overlapping proceedings exist, place every existing order on the record and ask for express adjustment directions in the order itself. A payer who lets an inflated interim figure run unchallenged for years is, in practical terms, funding sums that will never come back.</p>
</div>

<h2>Children\'s maintenance stands apart</h2>
<p>Whatever the outcome between the spouses, amounts paid for a child\'s maintenance are effectively beyond recovery. The child\'s entitlement is independent, the consumption presumption at its strongest, and no failure of the inter-spousal claim converts money spent on a child\'s food and schooling into a refundable overpayment. Settlements and final orders should therefore always state separately what portion relates to the child, so that later adjustment arithmetic between the spouses never touches it.</p>

<h2>The takeaway</h2>
<p>Paid maintenance behaves like water poured on soil: once absorbed, it is rarely retrieved. The payer\'s real protections are prospective — early contest, honest-disclosure enforcement, express adjustment directions and prompt applications under the alteration provisions when circumstances change. The payee\'s protection is equally clear: orders procured on true disclosures and spent on genuine needs are, in practice, safe from clawback. Both positions reward the same discipline — complete, documented candour about income and payments at every stage.</p>
